    {
        "content": "<p>In general, these classes would only be merged by mbtc, in the second example you show mbtc does trigger and is responsible for merging the classes (if you <code>grind -mbtc</code> the merge you are hoping for will not happen). </p>\n<p>The reason that grind did not attempt mbtc in the default configuration lies (to my understanding) in the heuristics that Leo chose for attempting to make an mbtc based case split. By default mbtc says: \"if <code>x = y</code> in the model of some theory solver (in this case the cutsat solver) we should attempt to propagate <code>x = y</code> into the e-graph and see what happens\". Leo restricted this with two additional constraints:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>the applications containing <code>x</code> and <code>y</code> must be roots of some equivalence class</li>\n<li>the applications containing <code>x</code> and <code>y</code> must have the same \"key\" where \"key\" is, as I understand, a simple first order pattern built from the surrounding application of <code>x</code> and <code>y</code> respectively</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The reason for adding this additional restriction is that mbtc would otherwise be too explosive according to Leo.</p>\n<p>In case 1 grind does not see an opportunity that matches restriction 1 and 2 and thus aborts before any mbtc can happen. In case 2, grind observes that <code>f 0</code> and <code>f (x + y)</code> have the same key and thus attempts to merge <code>0 = x + y</code> (as x + y happens to evaluate to <code>0</code> in the current model). So we then end up with <code>{0, f 0, 2 * x, x + y}</code> then by congruence we extend with <code>f (x + y)</code> and end up in the state that you are seeing.</p>",
        "id": 594316588,
        "sender_full_name": "Henrik Böving",
        "timestamp": 1778510358
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Is there a flag for changing this behavior? Or through an extension somehow?</p>",
